.SH NAME
tar  \- tape archiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tar
[ \fBcrtux\fIN\fBbflmqvw\fR ] [ \fIname\fR ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tar
saves and restores files in an archive. This is usually
on tape, but may be in a file archive.
Its actions are controlled by the
.I key
argument.
The
.I key
is a string of characters containing
at most one function letter and possibly
one or more function modifiers.
.PP
Other arguments to the command are file or directory
names specifying which files are to be dumped or restored.
In all cases, appearance of a directory name refers to
the files and (recursively) to subdirectories of that directory.
.PP
The default destination device is /dev/tardev, which is
created by linking it to a tape device.
If /dev/tardev does not exist, \fBtar\fR uses /dev/rct0 for
the default.
.PP
The cartridge tape unit has the capability of accessing each
of the four tracks on a tape individually.
The files 
\fI/dev/rct0a\fR, ..., \fI/dev/rct0d\fR, \fI /dev/rctla\fR, ...
refer to the individual tracks on a tape.
.SH OPTIONS
The function portion of
the key is specified by one of the following letters:
.TP
.B c
Create a new tape:  writing begins on the beginning
of the archive instead of after the end of the archive.
This destroys the existing files on the archive.
.TP
.B r
The named files are written
on the end of the archive
without disturbing existing files on the tape.
.TP
.B t
The names of the specified files are listed each time they occur
on the archive.
If no file argument is given,
all of the names on the tape are listed.
.TP
.B u
The named files are added to the archive if either they
are not already there or have
been modified since last put on the archive.
.TP
.B x
The named files are extracted from the archive.
If the named file matches a directory whose contents 
had been written onto the archive,
this directory is recursively extracted.
.sp
The owner, modification time, and mode are restored if possible.
If no file argument is given, the entire contents of the
archive is extracted.
If multiple entries specifying the same file
are on the tape (archive), the last one overwrites
all earlier files.
.PP
The following characters can be used in addition to the letter
that selects the function desired.
.TP
.B 0,...,7
this modifier selects the drive on which the tape is mounted.
The default is
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B b
the next argument is the blocking factor.
The range is 
1 to 20, the default is 8.  This option
is only used with raw magnetic tape archives (\fBf\fR below).
The block size is determined automatically when reading
tapes (key letters x and t).
.TP
.BI f " file"
the next argument is the name of the archive or file
instead of /dev/rct0.
Possible alternatives are: /dev/rct0, which is the
raw i/o tape device, or /dev/nrct0 for the no rewind device.
If the name of the file is \-, \fItar\fR writes to
standard output or reads from standard input, whichever is
appropriate. Thus,
.B tar
can be used as the head or tail of a filter chain.
.B Tar
can also be used to move hierarchies with the command
.sp
.RS
cd fromdir; tar cf - . | (cd todir; tar xf -)
.RE
.sp
.TP
.B l
print diagnostics if it cannot resolve all of the links
to the files dumped. If this is not specified, no
error messages are printed.
.TP
.B m
do not restore the modification times.
The mod time
is the time of extraction.
.TP
.B  q
do a quick extract.
It retrieves only the first occurrence of the file from
the archive and immediatly exits.
This is a modifier to the \fBx\fR option and must always be
used with the \fBx\fR option.
.TP
.B v
print the name of each file
preceded by the function letter.
With the
.B t
function,
.B v
gives more information about the
tape entries than just the name.
.TP
.B  w
wait for user confirmation.
If y is given, the action is performed.
.sp
If the '\fBx\fR' option is used with this option, then the user
can optionally give a filename (following the y answer)
for tar to extract into.  For example the following sequence
of statements:
.nf
.sp
     tar xw file.name
     x file.name: y file.name1
.fi
.sp
causes tar to put the file named 'file.name' from the tape
(archive) into the file named 'file.name1' in the present 
working directory.
.SH FILES
/dev/ct?
/dev/rct?
.br
/tmp/tar*
.SH SEE ALSO
ct(4).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Bad key characters and tape read/write errors.
.br
Not enough memory is available to hold the link tables.
.SH LIMITATIONS
There is no way to ask for the
.IR n -th
occurrence of a file.
.sp
Tape errors are handled ungracefully.
.sp
The
.B u
option can be slow.
.sp
The
.B b
option must not be used with archives that are
going to be updated (r or u option). The current tape driver cannot
backspace raw tape.
If the archive is on a disk file the 
.B b
option should not be used, as updating
an archive stored in this manner can destroy it.
.sp
The current limit on file name length is
100 characters.
.sp
If \fBf\fR is used with \fBc\fR, it will create the file 
specified by \fBf\fR if it does not exist.
